Setting up Neon as the volunteer-call database
==============================================

Neon provides free-tier serverless PostgreSQL. The free tier includes 0.5 GB
storage, auto-suspend after 5 minutes of inactivity, and ~1s cold-start on
reconnect. This is fine for a low-traffic volunteer coordination app.

Prerequisites
-------------
This walk-through stores the Neon connection string in GCP Secret Manager so
the deployed app can read it. That requires a working GCP project with the
Secret Manager API enabled. Run `docs/GCP_SETUP.howto` first if you haven't
already — concretely you need:

- gcloud CLI installed and authenticated (`gcloud auth login`)
- An active project set (`gcloud config set project <id>`)
- `secretmanager.googleapis.com` enabled (the `setup-gcp-project` script
  does this for you)

If you only want to point local dev at a hosted Postgres (no GCP, no deploy),
you can skip step 3 below and just set `DATABASE_URL` in your shell or a
`.env` file.

1. Create account and project
-----------------------------
- Sign up at https://neon.tech
- Create a new project (any region; us-east-2 is fine for Cloud Run us-central1)
- Create a database named "volunteer_call" with a role/user of your choice

2. Get the connection string
----------------------------
From the Neon dashboard, copy the "pooled" connection string. Today Neon
generates URLs like:

    postgresql://volunteer_call_user:password@ep-cool-name-123456.us-east-2.aws.neon.tech/volunteer_call?sslmode=require&channel_binding=require

For seeding via psql (libpq, step 4 below) the URL above works as-is —
keep both `sslmode` and `channel_binding`.

For the app at runtime (asyncpg), use the `+asyncpg` scheme and translate
the query parameters:

    postgresql+asyncpg://volunteer_call_user:password@ep-cool-name-123456.us-east-2.aws.neon.tech/volunteer_call?ssl=require

Two notes on what changed and why:

- `sslmode=require` becomes `ssl=require` (asyncpg's name for the same
  knob). Both mean "encrypt the connection, don't verify the cert". To
  verify the cert, use `ssl=verify-full` instead — Neon presents a valid
  Let's Encrypt cert, so this works.

- `channel_binding=require` is stripped from the asyncpg URL. Some
  background:

  Postgres clients negotiate one of two auth methods over TLS:
  `SCRAM-SHA-256` (plain) and `SCRAM-SHA-256-PLUS` (same, plus channel
  binding — which ties the auth to the TLS channel and prevents a
  MitM that's terminated TLS). Both libpq and asyncpg prefer the `-PLUS`
  variant when the server offers it, and Neon always offers it. So in
  practice channel binding happens whether the parameter is in the URL
  or not.

  What `channel_binding=require` adds is the *guarantee*: it turns
  "prefer `-PLUS`, fall back to plain if something goes wrong" into
  "fail the connection if `-PLUS` isn't negotiated". A safety net,
  not a different default.

  libpq has supported the parameter cleanly for years — keep it on the
  psql/seed URL. asyncpg only added support in 0.30+, and version drift
  to an older asyncpg would silently change the behavior. Dropping the
  parameter from the asyncpg URL costs only the fail-closed guarantee;
  the actual channel-binding protection still happens for every
  connection to Neon. If you need the protocol-level guarantee
  enforced by the runtime, pin asyncpg and re-add the parameter
  explicitly.

3. Store in GCP Secret Manager
-------------------------------
The asyncpg-flavored URL (with `channel_binding` stripped) is what the
deployed app reads at startup:

    echo -n 'postgresql+asyncpg://volunteer_call_user:password@ep-cool-name-123456.us-east-2.aws.neon.tech/volunteer_call?ssl=require' | \
      gcloud secrets create volunteer-call-database-url --data-file=-

To update later:

    echo -n 'new-connection-string' | \
      gcloud secrets versions add volunteer-call-database-url --data-file=-

4. Seed the database
--------------------
Use the plain postgresql:// connection string with psql — keep
`channel_binding=require` here:

    export DATABASE_URL='postgresql://volunteer_call_user:password@ep-cool-name-123456.us-east-2.aws.neon.tech/volunteer_call?sslmode=require&channel_binding=require'
    psql "$DATABASE_URL" -f scripts/initdb/01-schema.sql
    psql "$DATABASE_URL" -f scripts/initdb/02-reference-data.sql

(The local dev script `scripts/dev-db.sh` runs the same files against the
container DB; cloud seeding is the same files run via `psql`.)

5. Free tier notes
------------------
- 0.5 GB storage, 1 project, 10 branches
- Compute auto-suspends after 5 min idle; first query after suspend takes ~1s
- 191.9 compute hours/month (enough for always-on single endpoint)
- No charges; upgrade to paid if you need more storage or always-on compute
- See https://neon.tech/docs/introduction/plans for current limits

6. Upgrade path
---------------
When Neon's free tier becomes a constraint (storage, availability, cold-start
latency), see `docs/CLOUD_SQL.howto` for moving to GCP Cloud SQL. The only
runtime change is the `DATABASE_URL` secret value plus an
`--add-cloudsql-instances` flag on the Cloud Run deploy.
